<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Enshakushanna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 24th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/24th-century-bce">24th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Enshakushanna was a king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-dynastic-period">Early Dynastic period</a>, around the twenty-fourth century BCE, listed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a>. His inscriptions claim victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a> and the capture of its king Enbi-Ishtar, and he took the title lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a> and king of the land, an early claim to hegemony over southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/enshakushanna.jpg" alt="Enshakushanna" width="320" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hilprecht, H. V. (Hermann Vollrat), 1859-1925 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fragments_in_the_name_of_Enshakushanna.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
